  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nFriday, the LREF and global models meander the ridge to eastern \nArizona.  However, it's also forecast to strengthen, increasing \nlocal thicknesses to yield the warmest day this forecast as highs \nplateau ~ 7-9 F above normal, bringing most locations back into the \ntriple digits, if they aren't already there.  Additional Heat \nAdvisories will likely be needed.\n\nFriday night, a rare treat for this time of year is in store for \nWest Texas and Southeast New Mexico as am upper trough digging \nthrough the MS Valley sends a cold front towards the area, resulting \nin a chance of convection mainly in Southeast New Mexico.  \n\nThe cold front itself arrives either Saturday morning or early \nafternoon, depending on the model of choice, continuing chances of \nconvection Saturday afternoon and evening, mainly over Southeast New \nMexico.  For those who prefer cooler weather, highs Saturday \nafternoon should average ~ 2-3 F cooler than Friday, keeping most \nlocations in the double digits.  The front will continue pushing \nthrough the CWA Saturday night and Sunday, keeping highs Sunday \nwithin a degree or so of Saturday.\n\nUnfortunately, this \"cooling\" will be ephemeral, as highs most \nlocations bounce back into the triple digits most locations Monday \nand Tuesday, likely warranting more Heat Advisories.  Not \nsurprising, as this is climatologically the warmest time of the year \nfor these parts.",
